# --- Go / Wails build output ---
# Everything under build/ is generated EXCEPT appicon.png, which is the
# committed application icon Wails embeds into the Windows executable
# (generated once by `go run scripts/genicon.go`, BUILD-02 Phase 1g).
/build/*
!/build/appicon.png
*.exe
# ...except a vendored one. `go mod vendor` copies the Windows binary that the
# vendored Wails tree embeds (webview2runtime's
# //go:embed MicrosoftEdgeWebview2Setup.exe) into vendor/, where the rule above
# would keep it untracked: a vendored file is part of the SOURCE of a build,
# never its output. Without this negation a clean clone's vendor tree is
# incomplete and every Windows build fails with
# `pattern MicrosoftEdgeWebview2Setup.exe: no matching files found`, which no
# Linux job can see. The negation must stay AFTER the rule it negates: the last
# matching pattern wins.
!vendor/**/*.exe

# Wails may generate a frontend dist folder on some setups
/frontend/dist
# Wails CLI auto-generates JS/TS bindings here on every build; we call
# window.go.main.App directly from static/api.js, so these are never used.
/frontend/wailsjs

# --- Local session files (contain the re-identification key!) ---
*.anonsession.json

# --- OS junk ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Editor folders ---
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# --- UI test artefacts (docs/UITESTING.md layer 3) ---
# Screenshots and browser logs from the rendering harnesses. They are evidence
# for ONE run, uploaded by CI on failure and deleted locally on success.
/scripts/uitest/artifacts/

# --- Eyeball artefacts from the PDF redaction tests ---
# Renders written by the integration tier so a human can LOOK at what a
# redaction produced. Nothing reads them, every run rewrites them, and a file
# that reappears in `git status` after merely running the tests teaches people
# to ignore `git status`. Evidence for ONE run, like the UI artefacts above.
/backend/testdata/golden/

# --- Deterministic audit layer output (docs/audit.md) ---
# Tool binaries, raw tool output and SARIF files: evidence for ONE run,
# regenerated by `task audit`. Never committed.
/.audit/

# go-task's own checksum cache, used to decide whether `task tools` can be
# skipped. Machine-local state, regenerated on demand.
/.task/
